{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "\"the social network\" is a thought-provoking film that delves into the fascinating story of facebook's creation and the tumultuous journey of its founder, mark zuckerberg. directed by david fincher, the movie is a gripping narrative that explores the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg, a sophomore at harvard, being dumped by his girlfriend, erica albright. in a fit of anger and fueled by his intelligence and determination, mark creates a website called \"facemash\" that allows users to compare the photos of two students and vote on which one is more attractive. the site becomes an instant hit, but also generates a lot of controversy and gets mark into trouble with the university.\n\nsoon after, mark is approached by the winklevoss twins, who are part of harvard's elite social club, the porcellian club. they ask mark to help them create a social networking site for harvard students, called \"harvard connect.\" mark agrees to work on the project, but soon becomes obsessed with creating his own version, which he calls \"thefacebook.\" with the help of his friend eduardo saverin, mark launches the site, and it quickly gains popularity.\n\nas the site's user base expands, mark and eduardo's friendship begins to fray. mark becomes increasingly focused on expanding the site, while eduardo is more concerned with the financial and business aspects. the tension between them is palpable, and their friendship eventually disintegrates.\n\nmeanwhile, the winklevoss twins are furious when they discover that mark has stolen their idea and are determined to take him to court. the film's portrayal of the subsequent lawsuit is riveting, with the twins' anger and sense of betrayal evident throughout.\n\nthe cast delivers outstanding performances, with jesse eisenberg shining as the 